Z||000002||Sage||09-13-2001||11:16 AM||||Ken:<BR>Total agreement here.  He got slapped down during desert storm, and ran with his tail between his legs, but has been growling ever since.  He has had plenty of time to put everything in place to pull this off.  You don't have to be a genius to beat somebody into submission.  Besides, he himself did not have to come up with the details of a plan, he only had to get enough of the "right" people together, and fire them up to get behind his cause.  We should have taken him out in 90'.  He may well be the anti-Christ.  Unfortunately, I don't think we have heard the last from him.  <P>Please, no flames or challenges, this is just my opinion.  It is not fact, and there is nothing to debate.  The ball still hasn't landed, and I don't think we are done being "put in our place".  Keep in mind that terrorists do not have to come here to do their dasterdly deeds...they are already here.<P>Keep the faith, and try to have a good one.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Mach10||09-13-2001||11:38 AM||||It would not have been Saddam. He might as well have walked out in public and slit his own throat. Crazy and vile as he is, he's not THAT stupid. If there was ANY way for the US to pin what happened on him, he KNOWS that the US would turn his country into a glass crater.<P>Wow, you guys now have 53 states. One's in the middle east. Nothing there except a huge parking lot, a few oil wells, and a huge chunk of obsidian in the middle of which is Saddams charred, preserved carcass.||||reg||13
Z||000004||Ken Wittlief||09-13-2001||11:43 AM||||Also Saddam has been giving 'reward' money to the families of suicide bombers and palestinians killed while fighting against Israel - openly promoting the continuation of the violence and chaos in the middle east. $10,000 each or more!<P>Now the question is: Will Bush Part II go after Saddam? Will he take him out and invade Iraq - putting a stable governemt in his place?<P>Will he finish the job that was started in '91.<P>or is this all some sick twisted dance going on - will we play along and go after afganistan AGAIN - as if we dont know who is really behind this.<P>there is evidence that saddam was behind the first WTC bombing, the embassey bombings, the attack on the Cole<P>seriously! does anyone realize how absurd it is that saddam was left in power?<P>that we stand by and watch the back and forth squabbling between arab states?<P>and the killing between palestiniens and Israel?<P>We have the power and the authority to tell them:<P>STOP!<P>we have the might to make them STOP!<P>we have the moral justifcation to tell them to keep there religious fanatacism confined to their temples and mosques - if you want to kill each other in a holy war, go out and kill each other in the desert with your bare hands.<P>But if you want to be a part of the civilized world - if you want to sell us your oil and buy our goods, food, and technology, then you will be governed by the principles of BASIC HUMAN RIGHTS AND MORALITY<P>or you can go out in the desert and rot in the sun!<P>The civilized nations of the world have every right, and we have the means.<P>There is no excuse to allow this to continue - unless like I said - we are playing along in some sick twisted dance.<P>there is your connection to the dates: 1991 -&gt; 2001<P>its been ten years since the gulf war - early '91 to now.<P>happy anniversary Mr Bush<P>all the more sweet cause your son was in office at the time.<P>this is why Prez Bush has been so heart sick in the last few days - he knows part of the reason that thousands of americans have been killed is a PERSONAL attack against his father - and against him.<P>His fathers failure to take Saddam out of power has now resulted in a tragic attack on american soil.<P>_______________________<BR>from: <A HREF="http://www.worldtribune.com/worldtribune/breaking_1.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.worldtribune.com/worldtribune/breaking_1.html</A> <BR>Israeli intelligence: Iraq <BR>financed attacks <BR> <P><BR>GEOSTRATEGY-DIRECT.COM<BR>Wednesday, September 12, 2001<BR>Iraq recruited Saudi billionaire fugitive Osama Bin Laden and his Islamic allies to carry out the suicide attacks around the United States, according to Israeli intelligence.<P>Israeli officials and intelligence analysts said the suicide hijackings that downed the World Trade Center and destroyed parts of the Pentagon was too large an operation for any one group. The analysts said the operation was also too big even for a coalition of Islamic terrorists headed by Saudi billionaire fugitive Osama Bin Laden. Bin Laden is accused of masterminding the bombings of the U.S. embassies in eastern African in 1998.<P>Intelligence sources briefed the Cabinet of Prime Minister Ariel Sharon on late Tuesday, hours after the catastrophe in New York and Washington, Middle East Newsline reported. The sources were quoted as telling the Cabinet that a Middle East government was probably the sponsor of the attack.<P>The most likely sponsor for such an attack, the sources said, is Iraq. The Baghdad regime has long maintained an alliance with Bin Laden and Islamic groups.<P>"All the steps lead to him [Bin Laden]," Reuven Paz, a leading expert on Islamic terrorism, said. "Of all the countries, Iraq seems the most reasonable [candidate]."<P>In Baghdad, Iraqi state television appeared to welcome the bombings. The television said the spate of attacks demonstrated the vulnerability of the United States.<P>"The massive explosions in the centers of power in America, notably the Pentagon, is a painful slap in the face of U.S. politicians to stop their illegitimate hegemony and attempts to impose custodianship on peoples," the television said. "It was no coincidence that the World Trade Center was destroyed in suicidal operations involving two planes that have broken through all U.S. security barriers to carry the operation of the century and to express rejection of the reckless U.S. policy."<P>"The collapse of U.S. centers of power is a collapse of the U.S. policy, which deviates from human values and stands by world Zionism at all international forums to continue to slaughter the Palestinian Arab people and implement U.S. plans to dominate the world under the cover of what is called the new [world] order," the television added. "These are the fruits of the new U.S. order."<P>The Israeli Cabinet was informed that the United States might launch a massive attack on Iraq and Afghanistan over the coming days. The sources said such an attack could prompt a regional war.<P>On early Wednesday, Afghanistan appeared to have been a target of retaliation. Several bombings were heard in the Afghan capital of Kabul. U.S. officials denied that their country was responsible.<P>In the aftermath of the suicide bombings in the United States, Israel closed its air space. Israel also warned the Palestinian Authority to immediately stop all attacks against the Jewish state. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tonker||09-13-2001||01:08 PM||||Ken, <P>Iraq was my suspicion from the start. I don't know for sure whether they were involved but I can't see a more likely candidate in terms of motive, opportunity and ability. Bin-Laden might have been able to do this on his own, but my gut feeling is that this was just too well planned and organised. They're talking about something on the order of 50 people being involved now States-side.<P>Your points are all very well taken. One thing I'd like to suggest though is that you should look at who is supplying the evidence. <B>Israeli intelligence is one of the best in the world, if not the penultimate in espionnage capability</B>. Since the start I've been asking myself if the Mossad didn't have some idea of what was going on. They're pretty tuned in to what happens in the Middle East, and <B>I find it hard to believe that an operation of this magnitude escaped their attention</B>. If you want an intresting read about the Mossad, take a look at "By Way of Deception" by Victor Ostrovsky. He claims that the Mossad often will not share information with its international partners. By all means take the book with a grain of salt. He's a former Mossad agent, a Canadian. He seems somewhat jaded by his experience and he was kicked out of the organisation. A lot of what he says may be to earn some coin and get back at the Mossad.<P>Don't get me wrong I'm sure the Israelis as a whole are just as horrified by what happened as the rest of us, but take a look at what the Israeli government had to gain by this. Twice in the past two days Israeli forces have attacked the Palestinians in order to "root out terrorist factions". The know now that the US is not only distracted by its own problems, but probably also supportive of this action now. Israel can also count on more support against on going threats.  <P>Also, another sad and rather disgusting after effect of this tragedy is they way all the parties in Israel and the West Bank are using this to try to curry favour with the US. Both Palestinians and Israelis can be seen outdoors holding "signs of support" for the US in news coverage. These signs say something to the effect of, "We're sorry America, we feel your pain <I>and we're victims too.</I>".<p>[This message has been edited by Tonker (edited 09-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||ChadMan||09-13-2001||01:22 PM||||I saw former CIA operative Milt Bearden interviewed on CBS last night.  He worked in Afghanistan with the mudjaheddin from 1986-89.  He knew Bin Laden as a loudmouth, a good motivator of men and a good fundraiser, but not much of a military mastermind.<P>Bearden said he believed Bin Laden was probably involved, but that this operation was most likely funded and planned by a government hostile to the United States.<P>Sounds like Saddam to me.||||reg||8
